Two queues, not one:
  SYN queue     half-open; net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog (512 here)
  accept queue  established, awaiting accept();
                depth = min(backlog, net.core.somaxconn) [4096 here]

Overflow drops the ACK silently. ListenOverflows counts it.

int one = 1;
setsockopt(fd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_REUSEPORT, &one, sizeof one);
bind(fd, ...);          /* N processes may bind the SAME ip:port */
listen(fd, 511);

The kernel hashes the incoming 4-tuple to choose ONE listening socket, so
each connection goes to exactly one process. Linux 3.9+.

  one shared listen fd, N threads -> one accept queue, one lock
  SO_REUSEPORT, N listen fds      -> kernel hash, no shared lock

Caveat: adding or removing a listener rehashes, and connections already
queued on a departing socket are dropped. Drain before closing.

File to socket, naively: 4 copies, 2 context-switch pairs.
  disk -> page cache -> user buf -> socket buf -> NIC

sendfile(2): the user buffer disappears. (verified in-sandbox)
  ssize_t n = sendfile(out_sock, in_fd, &off, count);
  disk -> page cache -> (scatter-gather descr) -> NIC

splice(2): moves pages between two fds via a pipe, no userspace copy.
  splice(in_fd,     NULL, pipefd[1], NULL, n, SPLICE_F_MOVE);
  splice(pipefd[0], NULL, out_sock,  NULL, n, SPLICE_F_MOVE);

io_uring (IORING_OP_SEND_ZC) kills the syscall per operation.
DPDK kills the kernel, polling the NIC from userspace.
